WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.370 align:start position:0%
 
our national politics may seem  increasingly divisive but it's a  different story on the local level for  more than four years journalists James  and Deborah fallows traveled the country  and single-engine prop plane to learn  from Americans in smaller communities  the married duo visited places like  Allentown Pennsylvania Columbus  Mississippi Dodge City Kansas and Bend  Oregon in each place they met people  coming together to create jobs and solve  problems they Chronicle the experience  in their new book our towns a 100  thousand mile journey into the heart of  America James and Deborah fallows also  write for the Atlantic good morning to  both of you were so glad you could be  here this is a journalists dream to have  a fishing license to just go out into  the country so Jim what was the fishing  license at first what did you set out to  at first we just wanted to find out what  was going on around the country we've  been living in China for a number of  years and while they are we've gotten on  buses on the ox carts on trains to go  into the interior we thought what would  it be like to do that in this country  especially number one after the Great  Recession to see how people were  recovering and number two we had over  the years been to a number of these  places in our little propeller plane and  there are places you can get to that way  including that I own the airport in  Toronto where we've landed but they're  places you can get to in the Dakotas in  Hawaii etcetera so we thought let's see  what's out there  one thing I love by the way in the book  are the asides about flying which is a  great substrate but Deb tell me about  what surprised you the most in this  series of visits you know one of the  things that surprised me most actually  is the public libraries in all of the  small towns we went to mostly you  remember as a kid going in there to to  borrow books and read books but  libraries today have become something so  much more their social centers their  educational centers their technology  centers teaching people how to use  computers being the host for young  startup companies to get their  businesses going they are education is  primary for little kids and and literacy  programs for adults on the other end the  same thing the social and civic life  from teaching giving citizenship courses  are teaching ESL courses are doing yoga  or  or the tango and Jim this is you  developed an argument basically over the  course of these visits about what works  this isn't just oh these are nice  stories you you have a sense of what  works in these places and it's working a  lot better than in Washington yes we  have a sort of high level national  politics type argument and then a  practical city-by-city argument the high  level argument is at just the time when  we all know everything that's wrong with  the country and its national politics  and the real challenges the country has  from opioids to economic dislocation  city by city most people think they're  moving forward rather than backwards on  those issues and then we have certain  increasing patterns we saw of what made  that work one of those where I changed  my mind was the idea of a public-private  partnership which seemed to me just this  kind of slogan you'd hear a political  speech actually means something specific  in terms of schools or bridges or  orphanages or things that make these  cities better in fact you talked about  one former mayor Michael Coleman right  from Columbus Ohio and he convinced his  constituents that despite tough times  for a tax increase how did he do that  well this was right in the teeth of the  2008-2009 new depression which was as  hard in Columbus Ohio as any place and  the mayor had the choice does he layoff  very significant numbers of police  firefighters teachers etcetera or does  he ask his people to tax themselves when  they're losing their jobs to keep these  services going and they voted for the  tax increase in the city he's now one  that's just doing very well and one  other thing just as we've seen the  country change in terms of the loss of  manufacturing and mining jobs how what  did you find in these days about how  people are innovating we found a lot  especially with the Millennials give or  take a few years we're going back to  these towns and saying we want to be  here and we're going to take it upon  ourselves to start new companies to  start new endeavors and the one of the  interesting things also is that the  founding fathers in those towns said  these kids are what's going to make our  towns strong so we'll work with them  we'll lend them money we'll get them  going in places where they can do it  what I also loved was the question you  asked was who's who makes this town go  who is crucial to this town and that  that was a key to knowing whether a town  was going to be successful or not and  and who is crucial can  take all kinds of forms sometimes it's  the mayor sometimes it's the town  historian sometimes it's the musician in  town or a group of teachers in San  Bernardino it was the San Bernardino  generation now the Millennials who  started out as artists became kind of  Pied Pipers around town picking up trash  painting murals and ended up going into  the schools to educate the kids about  voting let's talk about the opioid  epidemic which is devastating this  country what did you find out when you  visited some of these communities can  they do it on their own or they need  help from the federal go so so number  one this is genuinely the worst social  problem US has right now we were seeing  early on we were up in places like by  Burlington Vermont which was exposed  this earlier than most others I think  this is a classic illustration where  local efforts can make a difference but  this is one of many fields where if we  had a more functional national  government that would be good too  because national programs make a big  difference but we found a number of sort  of city-by-city attempts to deal with it  but it's a genuine challenge are you  gonna keep doing this yes I didn't know  you were a pilot so that helps right  that helps getting to a lot of these  places  yeah I mean there's places you can get  to more easily by small plane than you  can by interstate and certainly by  commercial airlines and then another  great thing I love is the description of  the country from above because it's not  jumbo jet height right here you know the  changing landscape of the country it's  you know there's when when when flying  was becoming popular a century ago the  idea was that writers would want to be  pilots that all would want to because  you see the world students so uniquely  from 2,000 feet an airliner is too high  the road is too low but 2,000 feet you  can see the logic of the geography as it  comes together and the Norman Rockwell C  yeah such a great book thank you very  much thank you thank you we'll needs an  Deborah Fowler's we appreciate it  our towns goes on sale tomorrow

